Title: The Senator's Son
Order: 3
Production Code: 1x03

> FADE IN:

EXT. WINCHESTER - FAIRFAX BELGRAVE'S HOUSE - DAY

A costly residence, beautifully located. Mrs. Belgrave bought it the day before her son first entered the public school and installed herself as mistress. She receives no callers and pays no visits. She is a devoted Catholic; she attends every service of her church and rears Bernard in that faith.

SUPER: "THE TOWN SPECULATED ABOUT HER FOR A YEAR OR TWO, THEN GAVE UP."

INT. FAIRFAX BELGRAVE'S LIBRARY - NIGHT

The finest library in the northern section of Virginia. Books and magazines arrive by every mail. Fairfax Belgrave superintends her son's literary training. Politics, science, art, religion, sociology: the whole realm of human knowledge is invaded and explored. He values her company above every other pleasure.

INT. HARVARD UNIVERSITY - COMMENCEMENT - NIGHT

Bernard Belgrave, president of his graduating class and valedictorian, finishes an oration that sweeps all before it. The Associated Press telegraphs the news over the country.

EXT. HARVARD - THE DOOR - NIGHT

As he steps out to take his carriage, a tall man with a broad face and long flowing beard taps him on the shoulder and hands him a note.

INSERT - THE NOTE

In his mother's well-known handwriting:

"Dear Bernie: Follow this man and trust him as you would your loving mother. Fairfax Belgrave."

Bernard dismisses his carriage. He tells the man he is at his service.

INT. TRAIN - NIGHT

Bernard asks no questions, knowing that a woman as habitually careful as his mother does not send that message without due care and grave purpose.

EXT. WASHINGTON, D.C. - DAY

A carriage to one of the most fashionable portions of the city, to a mansion of splendid appearance.

INT. SENATOR'S MANSION - PARLOR - DAY

The tall man leads the way, having a key to which every door responds. Bernard is left in the parlor and told to remain until someone calls. The man with the long beard goes to his room and removes his disguise.

INT. SENATOR'S MANSION - REAR ROOM, SECOND FLOOR - DAY

A negro servant leads Bernard to a large room with two windows, one facing east, one north.

Facing him sits a white man, tall and of a commanding appearance. His hair, his whole noble head and handsome face bear a striking resemblance to Bernard's own. Bernard halts in astonishment.

The Senator rises and hands Bernard a note exactly resembling the first. He eyes Bernard from head to foot with keenest interest. From a drawer of his desk he draws a paper.

INSERT - The Marriage Certificate

A marriage certificate, certifying a marriage between Fairfax Belgrave and ------.

THE SENATOR
I am your mother's lawful husband, and you are my legitimate child.

Bernard knows not what to say, think or feel.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
I am Senator ------ from the state of ------, chairman of ------ committee.

Bernard rises from his seat with a bound. The name is a household word throughout the nation, his reputation international.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
Be seated, Bernard, I have much to say to you. I have a long story to tell. I have been married twice. My first wife's brother was Governor of ------ and lived and died a bachelor. He was, however, the father of a child, whose mother was a servant connected with his father's household. The child was given to my wife to rear, and she accepted the charge. The child bloomed into a perfect beauty, possessed a charming voice, could perform with extraordinary skill on the piano, and seemed to have inherited the mind of her father, whose praises have been sung in all the land.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
When this child was seventeen years of age my wife died. This girl remained in our house. I was yet a young man. Now that my wife was gone, attending to this girl fell entirely into my hands. I undertook her education. As her mind unfolded, so many beauteous qualities appeared that she excited my warm admiration.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
By chance, I discovered that the girl loved me; not as a father, but as she would a lover. As for myself, I had for some time been madly in love with her. When I discovered that my affections were returned, I made proposals, at that time regarded as honorable enough by the majority of white men of the South.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
She gently, but most firmly rejected my proposal. She told me that the proposal was of a nature to occasion deep and lasting repugnance, but that in my case she blamed circumstances and conditions more than she did me. A few days later she came to me and said: "Let us go to Canada and get married secretly. I will return South with you. No one shall ever know what we have done, and for the sake of your political and social future I will let the people apply whatever name they wish to our relationship."

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
Heroic soul! That is the marriage certificate which I showed you. You were born. When you were four years old your mother told me that she must leave, as she could not bear to see her child grow up esteeming her an adulteress.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
The war broke out, and I entered the army, and your mother took you to Europe, where she lived until the war was over, when she returned to Winchester, Virginia. Her father was a man of wealth, and you own two millions of dollars through your mother. At my death you shall have eight millions more.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
This infernal race prejudice has been the curse of my life. Think of my pure-hearted, noble-minded wife, branded as a harlot, and you, my own son, stigmatized as a bastard, because it would be suicide for me to let the world know that you both are mine.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
I want you to break down this prejudice. It is the wish of your mother and your father. I paid Mr. Tiberius Gracchus Leonard two thousand dollars a year to teach you at Winchester. His is a master mind. One rash deed robbed the world of seeing a colossal intellect in high station.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
I desire you to go to Norfolk County, Virginia, and hang up your sign as an attorney at law. I wish you to run for congress from that district. Leonard is down there. As you will find out, he will be of inestimable service to you.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
Now let me give you his history. Leonard was the most brilliant student that ever entered ------ University in the state of ------. Just prior to the time when he would have finished his education, the war broke out and he enlisted in the Confederate Army, and was made a colonel of a regiment. I was also a colonel, and when our ranks became depleted the two regiments were thrown into one. Though he was the ranking officer, our commander, as gallant and intrepid an officer as ever trod a battle field, was put in command. This deeply humiliated Leonard and he swore to be avenged.

INT. A CONFEDERATE CAMP - NIGHT (THE SENATOR'S STORY)

Night has just lowered her black wings over the earth. The two regiments are engaging the enemy. The commander is in advance of his men. Suddenly he falls, wounded.

THE SENATOR (V.O.)
At first it was thought that the enemy had shot him, but investigation showed that the ball had entered his back. It was presumed that some of his own men had mistook him for an enemy.

INT. THE SENATOR'S MANSION - REAR ROOM - DAY

THE SENATOR
Leonard had performed the nefarious deed knowingly. By some skillful detective work, I secured incontestible evidence of his guilt. I went to him with my proof and informed him of my intentions to lay it before a superior officer. His answer was: "If you do, I will let the whole world know about your nigger wife."

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
I fell back as if stunned. Seeing that each possessed a secret that meant death and disgrace to the other, for I shall certainly kill myself if I am ever exposed, I entered into an agreement with him. On the condition that he would prepare a statement confessing his guilt and detailing the circumstances of the crime and put this paper in my hand, I would show him my marriage certificate; and after that, each was to regard the other's secret as inviolate.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
His conscience, however, disturbed him beyond measure; and every evening, just after dusk, he fancied that he saw the form of his departed commander. It made him cowardly in battle and he at last deserted. He informed me as to how my secret came into his possession. He stole into my tent one night to murder me. I was talking in my sleep. In my slumber I told the story of my secret marriage in such circumstantial detail that it impressed him as being true.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
You see that he is a cowardly villain; but we sometimes have to use such.

THE SENATOR (CONT'D)
Now, my son, go forth; labor hard and climb high. Scale the high wall of prejudice. Make it possible, dear boy, for me to own you ere I pass out of life. Let your mother have the veil of slander torn from her pure form ere she closes her eyes on earth forever.

Bernard, handsome, brilliant, eloquent, the grandson of a governor, the son of a senator, a man of wealth, to whom defeat is a word unknown, steps out to battle for the freedom of his race.

EXT. NORFOLK, VIRGINIA - DAY

SUPER: "NORFOLK."

He arrives within a few days. He has decided to become the obedient servant of the people that he might thus make all the people his servants.

INT. HOTEL DOUGLASS, NORFOLK - NIGHT

A colored hotel where the colored leaders congregate. Bernard mingles freely and soon has the name of a jovial good fellow.

INT. NORFOLK - LAW OFFICE - DAY

Near the city court house, in a row of somewhat dilapidated old buildings, in a neighborhood infested by the rowdy and criminal element. Bernard has hung up his sign. Whenever any of them get into difficulties, he defends them; if they are destitute of funds he serves them free and often pays their fines. He goes on bond for any who get into trouble. He gives money freely to those who beg.

DISSOLVE TO:

INT. NORFOLK - CHURCHES - SUNDAYS

Though a good Catholic at heart, he divides his time among all denominations.

INT. NORFOLK - EVANGELINE LESLIE'S HOUSE - NIGHT

A reception by the reading circle, composed of the brightest, most talented young men and women of the city. Bernard, in company with a fellow lawyer, rings the bell. A young lady comes to the door.

She is a small, beautifully formed girl with a luxuriant growth of coal black hair arranged to give a queenly look to her head. Her skin is dark brown, tender and smooth. A pair of laughing hazel eyes. This is Viola Martin.

Bernard is so struck he does not greet her. He stares at her with a blank look. They are invited in. As he walks his head is turned over his shoulder.

INT. LESLIE PARLOR - NIGHT

Bernard and his friend in a corner.

BERNARD
Pray, who is that girl that met you at the door? I really did not know that a dark woman could look so beautiful.

FRIEND
You are not the only one that thinks that she is surpassingly beautiful. Her picture is the only Negro's picture that is allowed to hang in the show glasses of the white photographers down town. White and colored pay homage to her beauty.

BERNARD
Well, that man who denies that girl's beauty should be sent to the asylum for the cure of a perverted and abnormal taste.

FRIEND
I see you are rather enthusiastic. Is it wise to admire mortgaged property?

BERNARD
What's that? Is any body in my way?

FRIEND
In your way? Pray what do you mean? I don't understand you.

BERNARD
Come, I am on pins. Is she married or about to be?

FRIEND
Well, not exactly that, but she has told me that she cares a good bit for me.

Bernard sees his friend is teasing and leaves his side.

FRIEND (CONT'D)
(chuckling)
The would-be catcher is caught.

Bernard finds his landlady, a guest, and is introduced to Miss Viola Martin. He sits conversing with her all evening as if enchanted. She is highly educated, an elocutionist of no mean ability; she sings sweetly and is the most accomplished pianist in town. She is universally called Vie.

INT. LESLIE HOUSE - CLOAK ROOM - NIGHT

Viola goes up for her cloak. A half dozen girls follow and begin to pound her with pillows and smother her with kisses.

GIRL
You have made a catch, Vie. Hold him.

ANOTHER GIRL
He'll hold himself.

Viola answers with a sigh.

MULATTO GIRL
Theory is theory and practice is practice, eh, Vie? Well, we would hardly blame you in this case.

VIOLA MARTIN
I shall ask for no mercy. Theory and practice are one with me in this case.

MULATTO GIRL
Bah, bah, girl, two weeks will change that tune. And I, for one, won't blame you.

INT. LESLIE HOUSE - STAIRS - NIGHT

Viola comes down to be escorted home by the young man who brought her. He is jealous of Bernard and sullen the whole way.

EXT. THE MARTIN HOUSE - GATE - NIGHT

He bids her good-night in a most sullen manner. She does not notice. In her room she bursts into crying. She laughs a while and cries a while, as though she had a foretaste of coming bliss mixed with bitterness.

SUPER: "FOR A COUPLE OF YEARS: BERNARD AT HER SIDE. SOMETIMES SHE IS ALL LIFE AND GAYETY. AGAIN SCARCELY MORE THAN POLITE."

INT. NORFOLK - BERNARD'S OFFICE - DAY

Tiberius Gracchus Leonard, Bernard's old teacher, has been busy in Norfolk looking after Bernard's political interests under instructions from Bernard's father.

TIBERIUS GRACCHUS LEONARD
The time is ripe for you to announce yourself for Congress.

Bernard throws his whole soul into the project. He has another great incentive: Viola Martin's hand and heart.

EXT. NORFOLK - CAMPAIGN - DAY

The Second Congressional District, overwhelmingly Republican, always carried by the Democrats. Colored ministers support him. The white republicans fall into line. Viola Martin organizes patriotic clubs among the women.

SUPER: "BERNARD IS ELECTED BY A MAJORITY OF 11,823 VOTES. THE ELECTORAL BOARDS GIVE THE CERTIFICATE TO HIS OPPONENT, ALLEGING A MAJORITY OF 4,162."

INT. RICHMOND - DEMOCRATIC HEADQUARTERS - NIGHT

Leonard, who has made it appear in Norfolk that he is a democrat of the most radical school, bribes a clerk. Ballot boxes filled in advance, tabulated returns printed long before election day. He pays the clerk five thousand dollars for one of the tabulated statements.

INT. WASHINGTON - REPUBLICAN CONGRESSIONAL CAMPAIGN COMMITTEE - DAY

Fifteen of the most distinguished clergymen in the nation make affidavits that they saw the tabulated statement twenty days before the election. When Virginia's returns come in they correspond in every detail.

INT. THE HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES - DAY

Democratic by a small majority. A handful of democrats desert their party and vote with the republicans. Bernard's father has redeemed his promise of secret support.

The colored people hail Bernard as the coming Moses. "Belgrave, Belgrave, Belgrave" on every Negro tongue. Babies are named after him.

SUPER: "HE IS ADMITTED TO PLEAD BEFORE THE SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES."

CUT TO:
